   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Federation of Eswatini Business Community (FESBC) developed and adopted a new Strategic Plan (2020-25). To develop the Plan, FESBC conducted a perception survey with 75 FESBC members in July 2020. On August, FESBC presented the survey results at a retreat attended by 15 FESBC Board and Executive Committee members.
Based on findings of the survey and the retreat, FESBC identified four strategic focus areas as: Membership Management, Governance &amp; Coordination, Policy Advocacy and Finance &amp; Investment. FESBC&#x2019;s new Plan includes targeted approaches to improving members&#x2019; access to finance, timely and relevant information to run their business, and business management skills. The Plan also elaborates objectives to improve the governance and leadership of the Federation, and to provide tailor-made solutions to members&#x2019; concerns. To implement the plan, FESBC rebranded the organization and reintroduced itself  to members and potential members. 
Business Eswatini (BE) revised its constitution and other governance documents, and developed a new board Charter to monitor performance and reporting of committee members of BE in March-April 2021. In doing so, BE conducted a benchmarking exercise against other employers organizations in the region and internationally. The review revealed a number of areas for improvement, such as BE&#x2019;s large decision-making structures and duplication of work across departments. Along with the revised constitution, BE developed an implementation plan to ensure that transition to a new governance system is efficiently managed. 
The BE board adopted its revised constitution in July 2021. BE aligned its existing organisational policies and procedures with the provisions of the new constitution and amended terms of reference for Board committees. BE&#x2019;s new governance documents are aligned with King IV, a set of guidelines for the governance and operations of companies in South Africa which is generally adopted by organizations in the sub-region. The ILO provided guidance to FESBC in developing its strategic plan and undertaking qualitative interviews with key stakeholders including Board members, policy makers, and member associations and leading enterprise members. The ILO also assisted by analysing FESBC&#x2019;s situation, taking into account the global, regional, national and internal organizational contexts and constructing the long-term vision, mission, as well as specifically outlining strategic focus areas, for the next five years. Throughout this process, the ILO provided support in facilitating meetings with the senior management of FESBC geared towards the development of the Strategic Plan, guiding the development of the vision and mission, identifying strategic objectives, reviewing draft reports and monitoring the execution of the assignment.
For BE, the ILO provided full oversight in the revision of the constitution to ensure quality assurance throughout the process.  The ILO offered technical backstopping in the articulation of the scope, methodology and deliverables of both the benchmarking exercise and constitution revision, which ensured that the experts deliver to the needs of the organisation. In particular, the ILO assisted BE by   identifying model organisations for benchmarking, reviewing the draft reports as well as providing overall guidance in the transition to the new governance structures. Capacity support to BE staff was provided by guiding them on how to utilise the various governance documents such as the board charter, board member evaluation questionnaire and template.</narrative>
